  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAn area of light showers and sprinkles continues across central\nWisconsin, associated with mid-level warm air advection. This\nactivity will lift north through the evening across north-central\nWisconsin and become more widespread as better moisture arrives\nfrom the south. Abundant dry air in place and weak forcing will \nlimit the amount of rain that falls across the region, with less \nthan one tenth of an inch expected. High pressure then builds \nback in on Sunday, bringing a return to dry conditions across the\nregion to finish the weekend. \n\nA slow moving, moisture-laden system arrives Monday afternoon/\nevening and sticks around into at least Thursday. Rain is likely\nMonday night into Tuesday (80-95%) with the initial surge of\nmoisture and forcing. Then chance/likely PoPs will continue into\nWednesday, then taper off on Thursday as the system finally exits\nto our east. Another system lifting north at the end of next week\ncould continue to rain chances into Friday; however, this is less\ncertain as the models differ in how far west this system makes it.\n\nChances for exceeding 1 inch of rain Monday Night through Tuesday\nhas increased a bit to 60-90% north and west of the Fox Valley \nwith 40-60% over Fox Valley east to Lake Michigan. The probability\nfor 2 inches or more is also a respectable 30-60% across central\nand north-central Wisconsin during this same period. This set up\nappears to look like a soaking rain at times. Some elevated \ninstability and impressive dynamics with the initial WAA surge \nMonday night into Tuesday to include some thunder, especially \nacross central and north-central Wisconsin. \n\nTemperatures will be above normal through the middle of next week\nwith temperatures cooling off behind the large system at the end \nof the week.",
